<Base title="Accessibility" description="How Dotwear keeps VoiceOver, TalkBack, Dynamic Type and keyboard access working.">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Accessibility</h1>
    <p>
      Dotwear does not replace platform controls. It styles them. A Dotwear button in SwiftUI is still a
      <code>Button</code>, a switch is still a <code>Toggle</code>, and a text field is still a <code>TextField</code>.
      In React Native, buttons and toggles are a standard <code>Pressable</code> with the matching
      <code>accessibilityRole</code> and <code>accessibilityState</code>, and a text field is a standard
      <code>TextInput</code>. In Flutter, buttons are a standard <code>TextButton</code>, text fields a standard
      <code>TextField</code>, and toggles report <code>toggled</code> or <code>checked</code> like the standard controls.
      What the platform gives those controls for free keeps working:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>VoiceOver and TalkBack names, roles and states (for example “Switch, on”)</li>
      <li>Dynamic Type for labels</li>
      <li>Full Keyboard Access and focus rings</li>
      <li>Voice Control, text input and IME composition</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What we check</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Automated audits of the iOS demo app (<code>performAccessibilityAudit</code>) on every change.</li>
      <li>Tests of the role, name and state that each React Native and Flutter component reports.</li>
      <li>Touch targets of at least 44 pt on iOS and 48 dp on Android in React Native, and 48 × 48 in Flutter.</li>
      <li>Pixel-exact rendering tests for every icon on every platform.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Your part</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Give icons a label when they carry meaning on their own (<a href="/icons/usage/#accessibility-label">how</a>).</li>
      <li>Give icon-only buttons an accessibility label.</li>
      <li>Keep a visible label for text fields; a placeholder is not a label.</li>
      <li>When you customize colors, check contrast again.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</Base>
